\f0 \cf0 \
The fallibility of any advanced technology is inversely proportional to the intellect of the user, but even the most brilliant are prone to moments of colossal idiocy. It is the smartest, the highly educated and the best of breed who determine the ascent, decline or end of a civilisation.
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\ri-380\qc\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0  * * * 
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\ri-380\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 Thousands of transparent go-cubes, perfectly centred in their lanes and precisely one metre apart, silently careened above the broad bio-asphalt transit-way snaking a path through the lush urban rainforest. Dr Anthony Hardy squirmed uncomfortably in the synth-leather seat, frantically examining the holographic simulation floating in the air before him. According to reports, today\'92s test-run would proceed without catastrophe. The team had been running simulations for months and predicted a stable stasis field except, of course, for one annoying minority report. 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93A black hole?\'94 He finally said. 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Don\'92t worry, Tony.\'94 Dr Sarah Jacobson reassured with a flash of white enamel. \'93The probability is infinitesimal. I\'92ll bring up the numbers.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93No need. I\'92ve seen them, checked them, and checked them again.\'94 He terminated the projection and took his wife\'92s hand. \'93So where does this black hole come from?\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93A localised gravity well.\'94 She replied. \'93It is unexpected, but hardly catastrophic.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93A black hole is a black hole, you can\'92t dismiss my concerns so easily.\'94 He replied. \'93Exit ramp!\'94 
\f1 \

\f0 The cube jagged and took the off-ramp which decended sharply under the road-deck and into the heart of Marrickville Forest.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93How is it that no-one can make a go-cube with a smooth ride?\'94 Sarah squeaked, face smooched against the side window.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Old cars had wheels that actually touched the ground, amplifying every bump.\'94 Anthony grabbed her arm, reefed her upright and pecked her on the cheek. \'93No anti-grav back then.\'94 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93The world has you to thank for that.\'94 She beamed, although her contribution to dark-energy research was just as praise worthy. \'93We make greater strides today, do we not?\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Unless\'85\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93\'85 Unless earth gets sucked into a big black hole.\'94 She rolled her eyes. \'93But we do have the fail-safe protocol.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93That is of little comfort\'94 He countered. \'93We don\'92t even know if the information will be preserved.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Or if our transmissions will ever be heard.\'94 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Whatever happens, we will make history.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Or end it.\'94 
\f1 \

\f0 Suddenly decelerating the go-cube whipped 90 degrees and ducked into a narrow, un-signposted driveway snugly wedged between two straight rows of ancient, red mahoganies. The cube slid along the skinny boulevard, gliding into a thicket of eucalypts, pine trees and cedars gradually thinning out to a clearing of manicured lawn. The cube spun and paused. Two transparent gull-wing doors yawned open, allowing the two scientists to disembark. 
\f1 \

\f0 The pathway from the drop zone hugged the bank of a shallow meandering stream. Pristine water babbled quietly across flat pebbles, as the morning sunlight manically bounced off the rippling surface. A mob of grey kangaroos had gathered at the water\'92s edge, supervising their young who were jumping about in rough-house play, sorting out the junior pecking order. The largest of the males looked up and considered the two primates approaching, leaned back on his tail and displayed his broad chest which he scratch rhythmically with black paws.
\f1 \

\f0  
\f1 \

\f0  \'93Gotta pay the ferryman.\'94 Dr Hardy said as he produced a sandwich from his pocket. 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Is peanut butter even good for them?\'94 Asked Sarah. By now the animal was conditioned and bounded over, presenting himself for payment.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Does it matter? These things are everywhere,\'94 he said, offering up alms to the kangaroo king of Marrickville. \'93Not to mention the bloody koalas. How long did it take us to get here today? Stopping every five minutes for a fucking marsupial to cross the road.\'94 The kangaroo took the sandwich, a paw on both sides, took a bite and chewed happily.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Language, Dr Hardy!\'94 Sarah protested. \'93It makes you sound so dull.\'94
\f1 \

\f0 \'93We wouldn\'92t want to be wedded to a dullard, would we?\'94 He replied, sarcasm front and centre.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93You know what I mean. I would love you if you were\'85.a cleaner. But this is not about cleaners or koalas, is it?\'94 Sarah took her husband\'92s hand and they watched the kangaroo hop away. \'93The test will be fine.\'94 She kissed him gently on the cheek.
\f1 \

\f0 \'93You\'92re right, of course.\'94 He grinned, absently taking a draught of the cool morning air, imbibing the vapors of eucalyptus and pine. \'93It\'92s good to be alive.\'94 
\f1 \

\f0 \'93Let\'92s get to work then!\'94 Sarah replied.
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri-380\partightenfactor0

